{"kind":"request","text":"codex: design the retirement-succession safeguards for ratification — the three remaining preventions from hugh's withdrawn-backlog audit (2026-09-02), which found that of 502 withdrawn requests, real feature work (UI event addresses, the notification dropdown, the P1 browser-header item, and the staleness write-boundary fix itself) died in staleness-flare and lease-lapse retirements whose promised replacements never materialized: 281 withdrawn rows name no resolvable replacement and 61 more name replacements that themselves died. Design only; propose for ratification before any implementation request. Sequence after your current lanes; this is not urgent relative to the 4b12b30f merges.\n\nDESIGN THREE RULES AS ONE COHERENT PROPOSAL. (1) Successor lien on request supersession: retiring a request that never reached a satisfied closure must either name an already-filed effective successor request structurally (body.successor, projected as the successor_request edge the fold already keeps via linkedRequestSuccessor) or declare body.disposition abandoned with the author's reason — prose promises of a future refile become inadmissible at the write boundary, mirroring the succession rule artifacts already have. Decide and state the enforcement site, the admission error text, and the migration stance for the 502 existing rows (grandfathered, not migrated). (2) Broken-succession detector: a derived view in the projection and one board affordance that resurfaces a withdrawn-pending-replacement row whose successor is missing, retired, or whose chain terminates without a live or satisfied tip — the class that today is only discoverable by archaeology. Bound its cost; state whether it lands in needs-attention or as a qualifier on an existing population, following the reconciliation review's precedent (verdict 0ef41ca8) of qualifiers over new sections. (3) Lease-lapse discipline: an unclaimed request whose addressee has no live session is reassigned via reassign_if_unclaimed, never retired; state this as the rule the proposal ratifies, noting that the revived staleness write-boundary fix (my request d8b2b7a6, conditions carried from 8f7d36fd) removes the guard that currently blocks reassigning stale requests. Coordinate with, and do not duplicate, the merged-head reconciliation design lane (3491659b).\n\nCONDITIONS: a written design covering all three rules with enforcement sites, error texts, projection cost bounds, board surface, and migration stance; the design filed as a proposal for hugh's ratification before any implementation request; an exact-head artifact for the design note's path and independent review citing this request; explicit coordination statements against 3491659b and d8b2b7a6. If you judge any rule unsound or already covered, report that with reasons instead of designing around it and I will decide as author.","body":{"conditions":"One written design covering the successor lien on request supersession (structural body.successor or declared abandonment, enforcement site and error text stated, existing rows grandfathered), the broken-succession detector (derived view plus board affordance with bounded cost, qualifier-over-section per verdict 0ef41ca8 precedent), and the lease-lapse reassignment rule (reassign, never retire, noting d8b2b7a6 removes the stale-request guard); filed as a proposal for hugh's ratification before any implementation request; exact-head artifact for the design note published and independent review requested citing this request; coordination with 3491659b and d8b2b7a6 stated explicitly; any rule judged unsound reported with reasons for the author to decide.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}